WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.051 --> 00:00:03.392
Hello everyone! This is Artem Vorozhtsov.

00:00:03.392 --> 00:00:06.585
And in this bonus video, we're going to talk about the Fibonacci numbers and matrices.

00:00:06.585 --> 00:00:10.864
Let's recall how we defined the function F in our last video.

00:00:10.864 --> 00:00:12.864
Here are the first 20 Fibonacci numbers.

00:00:12.864 --> 00:00:14.523
They start with 1, 1

00:00:14.523 --> 00:00:16.551
Then come 2, 3, 5...

00:00:16.551 --> 00:00:19.886
Each subsequent number is the sum of the two previous ones.

00:00:19.886 --> 00:00:23.022
And this is exactly what we see in the definition:

00:00:23.022 --> 00:00:26.016
How to compute F[n_] ?

00:00:26.016 --> 00:00:29.862
We need to take F[n-1] + F[n-2],

00:00:29.862 --> 00:00:34.106
put everything into the dictionary F, and this is the memoization.

00:00:34.106 --> 00:00:36.561
Then return the resulting value.

00:00:36.561 --> 00:00:41.145
And separately in the dictionary F, 
values for zero and one are saved,

00:00:41.145 --> 00:00:43.173
so that this recursion is not infinite.

00:00:43.173 --> 00:00:46.751
SetAttributes[F, Listable] makes the function F "threadable over lists":

00:00:46.751 --> 00:00:53.608
if F is applied to a list, then we get a list of elements from the list,

00:00:53.608 --> 00:00:58.356
with function F applied to each element of that list.

00:00:58.356 --> 00:01:02.386
The idea of getting an explicit formula was this:

00:01:02.386 --> 00:01:06.170
Let's try to find a geometric progression

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:10.315
that satisfies the same relation as the Fibonacci numbers.

00:01:11.309 --> 00:01:13.394
Let's define ff.

00:01:13.394 --> 00:01:15.825
This is a geometric progression.

00:01:15.825 --> 00:01:19.515
There may be some factor b0 here, but it's not that important.

00:01:20.755 --> 00:01:24.168
The identity that is true for Fibonacci numbers

00:01:24.168 --> 00:01:26.939
here turns into this identity.

00:01:26.939 --> 00:01:30.849
It can be seen that it can be reduced by b0 and λⁿ.

00:01:30.849 --> 00:01:34.324
But we'll try to solve it directly.

00:01:34.324 --> 00:01:35.586
Yes, it can be solved.

00:01:35.586 --> 00:01:36.911
And look what happened:

00:01:36.911 --> 00:01:41.089
we found two values of λ that fit.

00:01:41.089 --> 00:01:44.631
That is, there are two values of λ

00:01:44.631 --> 00:01:49.915
such that λ^n fits this identity.

00:01:49.915 --> 00:01:54.000
The second element λ is called phi – the golden ratio.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.395
And the first element, which can be checked explicitly, is -1/phi.

00:01:58.395 --> 00:02:01.479
Lets check...

00:02:01.479 --> 00:02:02.339
True.

00:02:02.339 --> 00:02:03.840
Further the reasoning goes as follows:

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:09.091
We have two geometric progressions that satisfy this identity...

00:02:09.091 --> 00:02:10.808
...here.

00:02:10.808 --> 00:02:15.368
Then their sum will also satisfy this identity.

00:02:15.368 --> 00:02:19.178
It's not an obvious statement, but think about it.

00:02:19.178 --> 00:02:20.648
Moving on...

00:02:20.648 --> 00:02:23.855
Let's take these two progressions.

00:02:23.855 --> 00:02:27.598
One is "a" times phi^n ...

00:02:27.598 --> 00:02:35.765
... and the other is "b" times (-1/phi)^n.

00:02:35.765 --> 00:02:44.103
The sum of these two geometric progressions also satisfies the Fibonacci identity.

00:02:44.103 --> 00:02:50.434
So let's find values for "a" and "b" such that the zero-th element of this sum is equal to one...

00:02:50.434 --> 00:02:56.121
...and the first element of this sum of geometric progressions is also equal to one.

00:02:56.121 --> 00:02:57.317
Solving...

00:02:57.317 --> 00:03:01.998
The first argument of the Solve function 
 is a list of equations or inequalities.

00:03:01.998 --> 00:03:10.701
The second argument of the Solve function 
 is a list of variables to solve for.

00:03:10.701 --> 00:03:14.176
And it successfully solves it.

00:03:15.114 --> 00:03:16.511
Here are two curly braces...

00:03:16.511 --> 00:03:21.059
...basically, here could have been multiple pairs of "a" and "b", but we only get one pair here.

00:03:21.059 --> 00:03:26.234
We define the function F3 and verify that it works.

00:03:26.234 --> 00:03:30.613
Checking the 10th Fibonacci number, 
 we get the familiar number 89.

00:03:30.613 --> 00:03:33.753
Moving on. What I didn't mention in the previous video...

00:03:33.753 --> 00:03:36.177
...is that this coefficient...

00:03:36.177 --> 00:03:39.115
...and this coefficient can be simplified ...

00:03:39.115 --> 00:03:41.852
...or rather, more beautifully.

00:03:41.852 --> 00:03:46.500
Let's multiply the numerator and denominator by 5-√5.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:49.304
This is what we get for this case.

00:03:50.175 --> 00:03:52.816
Simplifying...

00:03:52.816 --> 00:03:55.221
The first coefficient is equal to this.

00:03:55.221 --> 00:03:58.395
The second coefficient is 2/(5+√5).

00:03:58.395 --> 00:04:01.970
We multiply again by 5-√5

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:03.836
and...

00:04:03.836 --> 00:04:08.852
It's clear that we get this.

00:04:08.852 --> 00:04:20.649
Thus, here's a more elegant expression
for the explicit formula of Fibonacci numbers.

00:04:20.649 --> 00:04:22.280
We check if it works or not.

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:23.476
It works.

00:04:23.476 --> 00:04:29.548
So, Fibonacci numbers are the sum 
 of two geometric progressions.

00:04:29.548 --> 00:04:35.536
And we know that the first number is approximately 1.618...,

00:04:35.536 --> 00:04:38.776
the second number is -0.618...

00:04:38.776 --> 00:04:44.443
The powers of these two geometric progressions
are drawn here.

00:04:44.443 --> 00:04:48.790
One has a magnitude greater than one, the other less than one.

00:04:48.790 --> 00:04:57.741
This term corresponds to the geometric progression 
 with a power of -0.618...

00:04:57.741 --> 00:05:06.957
When we raise -0.618 to the power of n, 
 we get smaller and smaller numbers with increasing n.

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:20.721
In the end, we know that the ratio of two adjacent large Fibonacci numbers is approximately phi.

00:05:20.721 --> 00:05:21.888
Not by chance.

00:05:21.888 --> 00:05:27.206
Because for large n we can kind of discard this term

00:05:27.206 --> 00:05:32.792
and then Fibonacci numbers are approximately equal to:
some number multiplied by phiⁿ,

00:05:32.792 --> 00:05:39.048
and the ratio of two neighbors becomes
equal to a number that is close to phi.

00:05:39.048 --> 00:05:42.959
The larger n is, the closer the ratio is to phi.

00:05:43.428 --> 00:05:49.097
Let's check that the function F3
correctly computes Fibonacci numbers.

00:05:49.097 --> 00:05:51.097
We've already checked that...

00:05:55.454 --> 00:05:58.658
What is a geometric progression.

00:05:58.658 --> 00:06:00.000
Example:

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:07.295
Let the zeroth element be 3,
and each subsequent one is the previous one multiplied by 2.

00:06:07.295 --> 00:06:14.192
3, 6, 12 - here's an example of an ordinary
geometric progression.

00:06:14.192 --> 00:06:17.832
And now I will write something scary 
 and incomprehensible here...

00:06:17.832 --> 00:06:22.246
"b" is such a row vector (1 0).

00:06:22.246 --> 00:06:31.116
"Q" is a 2x2 square matrix. 
 Two rows and two columns with elements 1 1 1 0.

00:06:31.116 --> 00:06:33.485
And we will write:

00:06:33.485 --> 00:06:34.781
b0...

00:06:34.781 --> 00:06:36.000
(Here is dot instead of multiplication)

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:41.862
b0.(q.q)

00:06:41.862 --> 00:06:46.049
One q here, two q here, three q here, four q here, and so on.

00:06:46.049 --> 00:06:47.044
What will we see?

00:06:47.044 --> 00:06:50.565
{1, 1}, {2, 1}, {3, 2}, {5, 3}, {8, 5}

00:06:50.565 --> 00:06:52.865
yes, quite familiar ...

00:06:52.865 --> 00:06:53.726
Here.

00:06:53.726 --> 00:06:57.100
Our task is to understand what is written here.

00:06:57.100 --> 00:06:59.033
Let's step by step.

00:06:59.033 --> 00:07:05.524
First, let's pay attention
that you can write this statement:

00:07:05.524 --> 00:07:09.102
a pair of neighboring Fibonacci numbers

00:07:09.102 --> 00:07:12.775
is written as a pair of such expressions.

00:07:12.775 --> 00:07:17.029
The last one is obvious.

00:07:17.029 --> 00:07:19.906
F[n-1] = F[n-1]

00:07:19.906 --> 00:07:25.190
And the first statement is simply the definition of Fibonacci numbers.

00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:27.626
This is true.

00:07:27.626 --> 00:07:32.575
But let's think about a pair of Fibonacci numbers,
as about a pair of x, y.

00:07:32.575 --> 00:07:36.641
And it's as if written here that

00:07:36.641 --> 00:07:46.359
a function of the old pair x, y
gives some new pair x, y.

00:07:46.359 --> 00:07:54.260
The general formula for obtaining a new pair x, y and the old one can look like this:

00:07:54.260 --> 00:08:05.778
new x = a x + b y
new y = c x + d y

00:08:05.778 --> 00:08:08.415
Okay.

00:08:08.415 --> 00:08:11.018
For Fibonacci numbers, we get:

00:08:11.018 --> 00:08:14.794
if the new pair {x, y} = {F[n], F[n-1]},

00:08:14.794 --> 00:08:19.844
and the old pair {x, y} = {F[n-1], F[n-2]},

00:08:19.844 --> 00:08:24.000
then from this expression

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:28.882
and recalling how it all looks like here,

00:08:28.882 --> 00:08:39.864
we get: a = 1, b = 1, c = 1, d = 0

00:08:39.864 --> 00:08:41.866
So...

00:08:41.866 --> 00:08:50.108
Next, we need the NewPointList function, 
 which is applied to the list of pairs x, y.

00:08:50.108 --> 00:08:56.295
Here we write a function that converts one pair of x, y and write that it needs to be applied...

00:08:56.295 --> 00:09:00.876
It is called map (/@).

00:09:00.876 --> 00:09:05.021
The hint doesn't show up,
but this is operator map (/@).

00:09:05.021 --> 00:09:09.367
We apply it to the list "l".

00:09:09.367 --> 00:09:13.780
Let's check how it works.

00:09:13.780 --> 00:09:15.151
Here...

00:09:15.151 --> 00:09:22.245
So, we passed a list of pairs as the first argument and got a list of transformed pairs.

00:09:22.245 --> 00:09:31.585
We could have added xx, yy
and gotten another transformed pair.

00:09:31.585 --> 00:09:36.150
Actually, we could have avoided introducing a new function, NewPointList,
and made the function NewPoint calculate
for both a pair (x, y) and a list of pairs.

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:42.809
Let's see how we can do this.

00:09:46.384 --> 00:09:48.619
Like this.

00:09:48.619 --> 00:09:51.893
This expression is quite complex and unclear.

00:09:51.893 --> 00:09:53.459
Here's what it says:

00:09:53.459 --> 00:09:55.985
We define the function NewPoint,

00:09:55.985 --> 00:10:00.000
then in square brackets we describe the arguments
and prerequisites for these arguments,

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.961
i.e., under what condition
the arguments should satisfy,

00:10:03.961 --> 00:10:10.089
in order to expand the function definition
as written to the right of the ":=" symbol.

00:10:10.089 --> 00:10:16.677
So, it says if x is not a list and y is not a list,

00:10:16.677 --> 00:10:19.614
then NewPoint is what is written here,

00:10:19.614 --> 00:10:21.415
i.e., the transformed pair.

00:10:21.415 --> 00:10:27.221
So we think of x as a number... about y.

00:10:27.221 --> 00:10:31.635
The second definition of the function NewPoint.

00:10:31.635 --> 00:10:35.854
We specify the prerequisite for the argument l.

00:10:35.854 --> 00:10:40.000
Prerequisites are written after the question mark.

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:49.753
MatrixQ is a function that returns True
when the argument l is a matrix,

00:10:49.753 --> 00:10:52.667
i.e., a list of lists.

00:10:52.667 --> 00:10:58.530
ListQ is a function that returns True
when the argument is a list.

00:11:05.684 --> 00:11:09.673
So this symbol, the exclamation mark "!",
means negation.

00:11:09.673 --> 00:11:12.951
That is, x_

00:11:12.951 --> 00:11:17.678
can be followed by either a type, a question mark, or a condition.

00:11:17.678 --> 00:11:20.501
Here we have a question mark and a condition.

00:11:20.501 --> 00:11:25.767
We need to describe a function that returns True or False.

00:11:27.272 --> 00:11:32.393
And if True is returned, then it's good.

00:11:32.393 --> 00:11:41.663
The definition to the right of ":=" is good
for computing the NewPoint function.

00:11:41.663 --> 00:11:43.379
Let's check how this works...

00:11:43.379 --> 00:11:47.140
This works as it should.

00:11:49.769 --> 00:11:55.952
By the way, note that if (a, b, c, d) == (1, 0, 0, 1),

00:11:55.952 --> 00:12:03.247
then we get ax = x, by = 0, so just x is here.

00:12:04.617 --> 00:12:09.506
c = 0, d = 1, so just y is here.

00:12:09.506 --> 00:12:12.947
Thus, the new pair x, y is the old pair x, y.

00:12:12.947 --> 00:12:14.425
Let's remember this.

00:12:14.425 --> 00:12:20.934
This combination of parameters a, b, c, d
gives us identical transformations.

00:12:20.934 --> 00:12:23.840
The next thing we will need is the Flatten function.

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:29.526
The Flatten function
erases all the curly brackets inside.

00:12:29.526 --> 00:12:33.068
It just makes a list of elements.

00:12:33.068 --> 00:12:38.989
The function F can be given a two- or three-dimensional matrix as input, and the output will be just a list.

00:12:38.989 --> 00:12:42.003
But if you specify 1 as the second argument,

00:12:42.003 --> 00:12:49.701
then you will get a list
with one dimension removed.

00:12:49.701 --> 00:12:55.366
And if you specify 2, then two dimensions will be removed.

00:12:55.969 --> 00:12:59.804
Well, if you specify 3, then three dimensions will be removed.

00:12:59.804 --> 00:13:06.396
In short, I want to draw this plot here.

00:13:06.396 --> 00:13:08.999
These are sets of points.

00:13:08.999 --> 00:13:11.472
A set of blue points.

00:13:11.472 --> 00:13:13.706
A set of yellow points.

00:13:13.706 --> 00:13:15.773
And a set of green points.

00:13:15.773 --> 00:13:18.802
Here I have blue dots,

00:13:18.802 --> 00:13:20.199
yellow dots,

00:13:20.199 --> 00:13:22.434
and green ones.

00:13:22.434 --> 00:13:24.300
Points are pairs of coordinates.

00:13:24.300 --> 00:13:28.713
And here, as I'm making this matrix,
what I end up with...

00:13:28.713 --> 00:13:29.808
The Table matrix.

00:13:29.808 --> 00:13:32.185
...I end up with a two-dimensional matrix.

00:13:32.185 --> 00:13:35.995
And I want to turn it into just a list of points.

00:13:35.995 --> 00:13:41.213
Let's Flatten this matrix
and we'll get a simple list of pairs.

00:13:42.319 --> 00:13:46.126
Here I have a pair of points 5i, j.

00:13:46.126 --> 00:13:51.812
In other words, "x" runs through the 5i points,
and "y" runs through the "j" values.

00:13:51.812 --> 00:13:57.901
So "x" kind of jumps immediately by 5,
and "y" jumps by 1.

00:13:57.901 --> 00:14:00.471
See, here are the coordinates 10, 20.

00:14:00.471 --> 00:14:04.180
Then, conversely, these become yellow points.

00:14:04.180 --> 00:14:10.656
And the third set of points this sun.

00:14:10.656 --> 00:14:14.032
Let's do the following...

00:14:14.032 --> 00:14:15.734
Here's our matrix.

00:14:15.734 --> 00:14:16.804
By the way, it's not like this.

00:14:16.804 --> 00:14:22.156
It's like this:
(a, b, c, d) = (1, 1, 1, 0)

00:14:22.156 --> 00:14:30.901
And let's draw a set of transformed
blue, yellow, and green points.

00:14:30.901 --> 00:14:34.422
The blue dots remain horizontal,

00:14:34.422 --> 00:14:37.729
but the yellow lines are now sloping.

00:14:37.729 --> 00:14:40.500
They were...

00:14:40.500 --> 00:14:42.098
No. Our blue dots have changed

00:14:42.098 --> 00:14:45.774
They were vertical and became horizontal.

00:14:45.774 --> 00:14:49.366
The yellow dots now go at a 45 degree angle.

00:14:49.366 --> 00:14:52.820
And as you can see, 
 the sun has become slanted and stretched out.

00:14:52.820 --> 00:14:55.590
Let's break this down.

00:14:55.590 --> 00:14:58.386
We'll put all of this inside Manipulate.

00:14:58.386 --> 00:15:06.251
Now a, b, c, and d are numbers 
 that we can change and observe what happens.

00:15:08.228 --> 00:15:14.148
Notice, I set a, b, c, and d equal to 1, 0, 0, 1, 
 which is the initial value.

00:15:14.148 --> 00:15:18.460
This is the identical transformation that I talked about earlier.

00:15:18.460 --> 00:15:22.841
The letter 'a', as we can see, 
 is responsible for horizontal compression.

00:15:22.841 --> 00:15:25.399
Compression or stretching.

00:15:26.706 --> 00:15:31.895
'd' is responsible for vertical compression or stretching.

00:15:31.895 --> 00:15:33.454
Okay.

00:15:33.454 --> 00:15:38.630
What about 'b'?
Now this is where it gets interesting.

00:15:43.089 --> 00:15:45.335
This is that slanting effect.

00:15:45.335 --> 00:15:49.805
And here's another slanting effect.

00:15:49.805 --> 00:15:53.906
You see, at some point we got a rotation.

00:15:53.906 --> 00:15:57.115
The squares remained squares.

00:15:57.115 --> 00:16:00.370
The entire plane just rotated.

00:16:00.370 --> 00:16:03.366
And also, apparently, it stretched out a bit.

00:16:03.366 --> 00:16:10.450
But if we bring "a" and "d" to numbers close to one,

00:16:10.450 --> 00:16:16.142
the image will look similar to the original, 
 just rotated by some degrees.

00:16:16.142 --> 00:16:18.198
Okay!

00:16:18.198 --> 00:16:21.676
Let's do the following operation.

00:16:21.676 --> 00:16:28.607
We'll apply the function "t" to the point 1, 1 several times.

00:16:28.607 --> 00:16:30.265
{13, 8}

00:16:30.265 --> 00:16:35.048
We recognize the Fibonacci numbers.

00:16:35.048 --> 00:16:41.763
This thing, applying the function several times, is called Nest in Mathematica.

00:16:41.763 --> 00:16:44.785
Mathematica is a functional programming language.

00:16:44.785 --> 00:16:49.506
The function Nest takes the function h as a first argument,

00:16:49.506 --> 00:16:56.306
The second argument is x, to which the function h should be applied,

00:16:56.306 --> 00:16:59.243
and the third argument is how many times.

00:17:00.208 --> 00:17:04.498
NestList gives us the successive steps of applying the function h.

00:17:05.322 --> 00:17:06.295
There.

00:17:06.948 --> 00:17:07.840
There.

00:17:08.436 --> 00:17:13.131
Thus, we have another way of computing Fibonacci numbers.

00:17:13.131 --> 00:17:18.198
Let's draw the result of executing NestList.

00:17:19.646 --> 00:17:21.396
Here.

00:17:22.646 --> 00:17:26.869
The coordinates of these points are the adjacent Fibonacci numbers.

00:17:27.724 --> 00:17:30.430
Now let's take the logarithm.

00:17:31.834 --> 00:17:35.158
It starts from point (0, 0) – which is (log 1, log 1).

00:17:35.158 --> 00:17:43.959
Then the coordinates are equal 
 to the (log 2, 0), then (log 3, log 2) and so on. 

00:17:43.959 --> 00:17:47.568
It is evident that further points lie approximately on a straight line.

00:17:47.568 --> 00:17:53.690
This is the case of convergence to a geometric progression.

00:17:53.690 --> 00:18:00.080
And let's try to see what else we could get 
 if we took other numbers a, b, c, d.

00:18:01.856 --> 00:18:04.144
This is exactly the case described here.

00:18:04.144 --> 00:18:05.139
So...

00:18:05.139 --> 00:18:08.144
Some numbers a, b, c, d are taken here...

00:18:08.144 --> 00:18:12.557
n is how many times it needs to be applied.

00:18:14.300 --> 00:18:15.374
"a"...

00:18:16.212 --> 00:18:19.553
We start gradually changing "a"...

00:18:19.553 --> 00:18:21.352
slowly...

00:18:21.990 --> 00:18:26.818
n is the number of steps. Let's increase this number.

00:18:28.125 --> 00:18:29.610
Here...

00:18:30.528 --> 00:18:32.568
Changing "a"...

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:37.131
This is a diverging spiral.

00:18:37.131 --> 00:18:43.232
When we decrease n, the outer links begin to disappear.

00:18:43.232 --> 00:18:45.869
Now let's increase it again.

00:18:49.120 --> 00:18:54.350
And this is how the inner links disappear.

00:18:59.076 --> 00:19:02.528
"с"...

00:19:02.528 --> 00:19:05.198
Well... a similar effect.

00:19:17.566 --> 00:19:22.486
They all more or less make the same picture.

00:19:24.698 --> 00:19:29.531
But we have already obtained some strange cases here.

00:19:38.312 --> 00:19:44.276
No longer spirals, but such strange ones...

00:19:45.382 --> 00:19:48.462
Alright. Let's see how our viewer is doing.

00:19:49.772 --> 00:19:52.826
Our viewer's hair is standing on end.

00:19:53.697 --> 00:19:57.239
Well... good. That's the expected effect.

00:19:57.608 --> 00:19:59.533
Moving on.

00:20:00.740 --> 00:20:04.095
Let's apply this transformation twice.

00:20:04.095 --> 00:20:05.357
Or better yet:

00:20:05.357 --> 00:20:08.262
once with coefficients a1, b1, c1, d1,

00:20:08.262 --> 00:20:11.804
and once with coefficients a2, b2, c2, d2.

00:20:11.804 --> 00:20:13.533
What do we get?

00:20:13.533 --> 00:20:15.298
We get this expression.

00:20:15.298 --> 00:20:19.946
Using the Collect function, 
 we can gather the coefficients for x and y.

00:20:19.946 --> 00:20:20.974
Here...

00:20:20.974 --> 00:20:30.515
We see that the double application of the NewPoint transformation gives us a transformation of the same type,

00:20:30.515 --> 00:20:35.934
where a = a1a2+b2c1,

00:20:35.934 --> 00:20:41.256
the new b = a2b1+b2d1, and so on.

00:20:41.256 --> 00:20:42.887
So...

00:20:43.624 --> 00:20:44.470
Well, what...

00:20:44.470 --> 00:20:46.278
This is what is called matrix multiplication.

00:20:46.278 --> 00:20:48.785
This set of coefficients for matrix 1,

00:20:48.785 --> 00:20:51.752
this set of coefficients for matrix 2,

00:20:51.752 --> 00:20:59.450
and this new set of coefficients a, b, c, d
is the result of multiplying matrices.

00:21:00.489 --> 00:21:04.685
I think it's very clear where this comes from.

00:21:04.685 --> 00:21:10.280
We want to apply these 
 stretches and shears one after the other.

00:21:10.280 --> 00:21:18.882
One transformation has certain coefficients a1, b1, c1, d1, 
 while the other has different ones.

00:21:18.882 --> 00:21:26.712
What will be the coefficients of the transformation obtained by applying sequentially, firstly the first one, then the second?

00:21:26.712 --> 00:21:28.162
Here's the answer.

00:21:28.162 --> 00:21:37.366
And in Mathematica, this answer is already built into the operator for multiplying two matrices.

00:21:37.366 --> 00:21:40.434
The first matrix is a1, b1, c1, d1.

00:21:40.434 --> 00:21:42.559
Here's the second matrix.

00:21:42.559 --> 00:21:49.988
We multiply them and then ask
to write this in matrix form.

00:21:49.988 --> 00:21:51.988
It seems to match.

00:21:55.541 --> 00:21:57.713
Here's our matrix.

00:21:57.713 --> 00:22:00.584
Let's multiply it by itself several times.

00:22:00.584 --> 00:22:04.897
We'll find the numbers 34, 21.

00:22:04.897 --> 00:22:15.542
In mathematics, there's a function called MatrixPower, which allows you to compute the product of 8 such matrices.

00:22:15.542 --> 00:22:17.138
Okay.

00:22:17.149 --> 00:22:20.893
And here's our fourth definition of Fibonacci numbers.

00:22:20.893 --> 00:22:28.490
To get MatrixPower (1, 1, 1, 0),
we take the first element of the first row.

00:22:28.490 --> 00:22:34.009
This is like the first row,
and 34 is the first element in this case.

00:22:34.009 --> 00:22:35.875
Let's check how it works.

00:22:35.875 --> 00:22:37.540
It just works!

00:22:37.540 --> 00:22:39.674
How long does it take to work?

00:22:39.674 --> 00:22:41.326
Very fast.

00:22:41.326 --> 00:22:43.205
But let's compare anyway.

00:22:43.205 --> 00:22:47.015
First, let's try to define the MatrixPower function ourselves.

00:22:47.015 --> 00:22:49.920
Let's call it MatrixPower2.

00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:51.417
How will we define it?

00:22:51.417 --> 00:22:55.629
For n=0, we'll return the matrix
that is equal to one.

00:22:55.629 --> 00:23:00.843
As usual for aⁿ.
If n = 0, then one must be returned.

00:23:00.854 --> 00:23:04.439
And here we act in the world of matrices,
so we need to return the identity matrix.

00:23:04.439 --> 00:23:07.679
The identity transformation matrix.

00:23:07.679 --> 00:23:14.413
If n &gt; 0, then we return m * m^(n-1).

00:23:14.413 --> 00:23:18.022
Let's check if this definition works.

00:23:18.022 --> 00:23:19.868
It seems to work.

00:23:19.868 --> 00:23:21.331
And it's quite fast.

00:23:21.331 --> 00:23:23.599
What about two thousand?

00:23:23.599 --> 00:23:26.409
Not instant anymore.

00:23:26.409 --> 00:23:29.783
It takes 0.038 seconds.

00:23:31.124 --> 00:23:34.752
Let's define mⁿ more efficiently.

00:23:34.752 --> 00:23:40.506
This is a very important code. The classic one.

00:23:40.506 --> 00:23:42.197
Let's analyse it.

00:23:42.197 --> 00:23:50.036
If n = 0, we return the identity matrix as before. The identity transformation matrix.

00:23:50.807 --> 00:23:52.446
And here's the interesting part...

00:23:52.446 --> 00:23:54.678
Here's an old option.

00:23:54.678 --> 00:24:00.826
If "n" modulo 2 (i.e. the remainder of n divided by 2) equals 0,

00:24:00.826 --> 00:24:02.777
then we do this.

00:24:02.777 --> 00:24:05.380
If "n" is odd, then it's this.

00:24:05.380 --> 00:24:09.424
So for odd "n", we do the old way.

00:24:09.424 --> 00:24:15.446
That is, we recursively call mⁿ⁻¹ and multiply it by m.

00:24:15.446 --> 00:24:26.478
And if "n" is even, we multiply "m" by itself and square the matrix m to the power of n/2.

00:24:26.478 --> 00:24:33.932
This is a cool idea if you need
3 * 3 * 3 * 3 * 3 * 3.

00:24:33.932 --> 00:24:36.535
You can do this...

00:24:36.535 --> 00:24:39.282
You can multiply three by itself six times,

00:24:39.282 --> 00:24:47.985
or you can do 3 * 3, and then multiply the result of 3 * 3 three times.

00:24:47.985 --> 00:24:52.081
Multiply three numbers,
that is, choose two multiplication operators.

00:24:52.081 --> 00:24:54.081
Of course, with the example of six, it's not that impressive,

00:24:54.081 --> 00:24:58.666
but imagine if you have 3²⁵⁶.

00:24:58.666 --> 00:25:03.749
So then you probably quickly understand that

00:25:03.749 --> 00:25:12.653
(3*3)^128 – is the big leap!  Not forward, but backwards. We are going to zero, not to the infinity.

00:25:12.653 --> 00:25:17.904
When "n" equals zero, we'll know the answer and return it immediately.

00:25:17.904 --> 00:25:21.739
We need to reduce "n" as quickly as possible using recursion.

00:25:21.739 --> 00:25:26.253
And this is a good way to reduce it. We half number n here.

00:25:26.253 --> 00:25:27.550
Okay...

00:25:28.086 --> 00:25:29.617
I know, it is quite vague explanation...

00:25:29.617 --> 00:25:36.121
Read it carefully, meditate on it, and you'll understand.

00:25:36.121 --> 00:25:37.217
It works.

00:25:38.088 --> 00:25:40.586
But how fast does it work?

00:25:40.586 --> 00:25:44.819
Oh! It takes only 0.007 seconds. 
 Do you remember how long it was before?

00:25:44.819 --> 00:25:50.137
It was 0.03-0.04 seconds.

00:25:54.695 --> 00:26:01.845
Yes. The unoptimized version returns around 0.03-0.04.

00:26:01.845 --> 00:26:05.897
And here it is for 10000.

00:26:05.897 --> 00:26:08.199
But the numbers here are very large.

00:26:08.199 --> 00:26:11.050
Let's do a trick.

00:26:11.050 --> 00:26:18.811
Let's take it modulo 1597 in order not too much returns.

00:26:20.621 --> 00:26:22.340
There we go.

00:26:22.943 --> 00:26:27.725
And let's compare it with the built-in function MatrixPower.

00:26:27.725 --> 00:26:29.356
Oops! We've beaten it.

00:26:29.356 --> 00:26:31.189
Again...

00:26:32.865 --> 00:26:36.424
We've beaten it again! We're great.

00:26:36.424 --> 00:26:42.466
We've given such a definition to MatrixPower2o 
(o – stands for optimized)

00:26:42.466 --> 00:26:45.941
that we've beaten the built-in MatrixPower function.

00:26:45.941 --> 00:26:49.110
Well, that's it for today.

00:26:49.110 --> 00:26:55.970
Of course, I haven't told you everything about matrices yet...

00:26:55.970 --> 00:26:59.444
A very important point, of course, I haven't mentioned.

00:26:59.444 --> 00:27:03.601
In this algorithm for computing matrices...

00:27:03.601 --> 00:27:06.149
Alright, let me delete this.

00:27:06.149 --> 00:27:09.366
...a very important property is explicitly used.

00:27:09.366 --> 00:27:11.064
Namely...

00:27:11.064 --> 00:27:13.902
Where are these matrices? Let me copy them.

00:27:13.902 --> 00:27:15.432
Here they are.

00:27:15.432 --> 00:27:19.778
...a very important property of matrices is:

00:27:19.778 --> 00:27:21.982
that I can group them however I want.

00:27:21.982 --> 00:27:26.639
I can group them like this.

00:27:26.639 --> 00:27:29.477
Like so...

00:27:31.366 --> 00:27:38.326
And the result doesn't depend on how I group them.

00:27:39.734 --> 00:27:41.250
This is a challenge.

00:27:41.250 --> 00:27:43.250
Try proving it yourself.

00:27:43.250 --> 00:27:48.680
This property is called the associative 
 property of matrix multiplication.

00:27:48.680 --> 00:27:53.001
Also, I didn't mention that matrices can be not only 2x2, 
 but also 3x3.

00:27:53.001 --> 00:27:54.364
But you can figure that out on your own.

00:27:54.364 --> 00:27:57.795
Instead of x, y and the transformation of x, y to a new x, y,

00:27:57.795 --> 00:28:02.217
we need to take the transformation of x, y, z.

00:28:02.217 --> 00:28:08.620
Task: write down the formula for multiplying 3x3 matrices.

00:28:08.620 --> 00:28:17.537
Ask Mathematica to perform the same operation we did.

00:28:17.537 --> 00:28:20.140
Here it is.

00:28:20.940 --> 00:28:24.120
Apply Collect  on expanded expression.

00:28:24.120 --> 00:28:26.388
You will have x, y, z here.

00:28:26.388 --> 00:28:32.410
And you need to see what the coefficients 
 are in each row for x, y and z.

00:28:33.382 --> 00:28:35.013
Well, in general...

00:28:35.013 --> 00:28:38.253
there are many ideas for independent work,

00:28:38.253 --> 00:28:42.845
for independent mathematical experiments.

00:28:42.845 --> 00:28:45.884
Let's see how our viewer is doing.

00:28:45.884 --> 00:28:48.460
Great! Good bye!

